# Line endings for repo-owned PowerShell. LF matches what is committed today
# (core.autocrlf=input), so this only guards against a future autocrlf change
# rewriting these files wholesale.
/init.ps1        text eol=lf
/scripts/*.ps1   text eol=lf

# init.ps1 must keep its UTF-8 BOM.
#
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 falls back to CP1252 for BOM-less files. The check-mark
# characters (U+2713) in this file then decode as three CP1252 characters, the
# last of which is U+201C LEFT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK -- and PS 5.1 accepts a
# smart quote as a string delimiter. Each one therefore opens a string that
# swallows every line until the next, which surfaces as a bogus
# "Missing closing '}'" error pointing at an unrelated function.
# PowerShell 7 defaults to UTF-8 and is unaffected, so this hides easily.
#
# git CANNOT enforce the BOM: working-tree-encoding=UTF-8-BOM is rejected
# ("fatal: failed to encode ... from UTF-8-BOM to UTF-8" on git 2.52) and a
# plain working-tree-encoding=UTF-8 is a no-op. Enforcement lives in
# .editorconfig (charset = utf-8-bom). Keep the two files in sync.
